WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Are you afraid of heights? Do you hyperventilate at the thought of a flu shot? Do you go out of your way to avoid elevators and other small enclosed spaces? Have you driven across the country because you're too frightened to fly? Or avoided a particular driving route because you're terrified of bridges? All of these are examples of what psychologists call specific phobias, which constitute one of the most common forms of anxiety disorder. studies have found that anywhere from 9% to 13% of people experience a specific phobia at some point in their lives.
您有恐高症吗？一想到打流感疫苗就会过度换气吗？您是否会特意避开电梯和其他狭小的封闭空间？您是否因为害怕飞行而选择横跨全国的自驾游？或者因为极度恐惧桥梁而避开特定的行车路线？所有这些都是心理学家所称的“特定恐惧症”的例子，它是焦虑症中最常见的形式之一。研究发现，9% 到 13% 的人在其一生中的某个阶段会经历特定恐惧症。

2
00:00:38.000 --> 00:01:13.000
And unfortunately, although effective treatments are available, far fewer people receive treatment than could benefit from it. So what are phobias and how do they differ from fears? What are some of the most common phobias and how common are they? Where do phobias come from? Are you born with them or are they learned? What treatments are available for phobias and how well do they work? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life.
遗憾的是，尽管目前已有有效的治疗方法，但获得治疗的人数远少于能够从中受益的人数。那么，什么是恐惧症？它与恐惧有何不同？最常见的恐惧症有哪些？它们的普遍程度如何？恐惧症从何而来？是天生的还是后天习得的？有哪些治疗恐惧症的方法？效果如何？欢迎收听《心理学谈话》（Speaking of Psychology），这是美国心理学会的旗舰播客，旨在探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。

3
00:01:13.000 --> 00:02:05.000
I'm Kim Mills. My guest today is Dr. Martin Antony, a professor in the Department of Psychology at Toronto Metropolitan University. He also holds part-time or adjunct faculty appointments at McMaster University and the University of Toronto, and he was the founding director of the Anxiety Treatment and Research Clinic at St. Joseph's Healthcare in Hamilton, Ontario. Dr. Anthony is internationally known for his books and research on anxiety disorders, including phobias, as well as perfectionism, cognitive behavioral therapy, and assessment of psychological disorders. He has published 33 books, including several evidence-based self-help manuals for the general public on overcoming phobias and anxiety.
我是主持人金·米尔斯（Kim Mills）。今天的嘉宾是多伦多都会大学心理学系教授马丁·安东尼（Dr. Martin Antony）博士。他还在麦克马斯特大学和多伦多大学担任兼职或客座教职，并曾是安大略省汉密尔顿市圣约瑟夫医疗中心焦虑治疗与研究诊所的创始主任。安东尼博士因其在焦虑症（包括恐惧症）、完美主义、认知行为疗法以及心理障碍评估方面的著作和研究而享誉国际。他已出版了 33 本书，其中包括多本面向大众、基于证据的自助手册，旨在帮助人们克服恐惧症和焦虑。

4
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Dr. Antony is a fellow of the Royal Society of Canada and a past president of the Canadian Psychological Association. Thank you for joining me today, Dr. Anthony. My pleasure. Thank you for inviting me to be here. Let's start by talking about the difference between a fear and a phobia. A phobia is just a more extreme type of fear, or are they more complex than that? So a phobia is a fear that, first of all, is excessive. So it's out of proportion to the actual danger in the situation. But it's also a fear that is causing problems for a person.
安东尼博士是加拿大皇家学会会员，也是加拿大心理学会的前任主席。感谢您今天参加我们的节目，安东尼博士。不客气，很高兴受邀来到这里。让我们先从恐惧和恐惧症的区别谈起。恐惧症只是恐惧的一种极端表现，还是说它们比这更复杂？恐惧症首先是一种过度的恐惧，即与情境中的实际危险不成比例。但同时，这种恐惧也正在给当事人造成困扰。

5
00:02:41.000 --> 00:03:06.000
So it's maybe bothering them that they have the fear. Or it's interfering with their life in some way. So you may be terrified of snakes. But if you never see a snake, it doesn't really affect what you do in your day-to-day life. Then who cares? You just have a fear of snakes. On the other hand, if you can't leave your house because you might see a snake, and your fear of snakes is affecting your ability to enjoy a movie or a TV show
也就是说，这种恐惧可能让他们感到苦恼，或者在某种程度上干扰了他们的生活。比如，你可能非常害怕蛇，但如果你从不接触蛇，这并不会真正影响你的日常生活，那又有什么关系呢？你只是对蛇有恐惧感。另一方面，如果你因为可能看到蛇而无法出门，或者你对蛇的恐惧已经影响到你欣赏电影或电视节目的能力，

6
00:03:06.000 --> 00:04:11.000
because there might be a snake on there, or you're having nightmares about snakes that are keeping you awake, then it might be at a level that we might call a phobia. So that's one common phobia, snakes, but what are some of the other common phobias and what are the triggers for these phobias? Is there something that ties them together, particularly? Yeah, so the most common phobia is most likely animal phobias. So spider, snakes, dogs, cats, birds, mice. And often among animal phobias, the ones that are most common are the fears of creepy, crawly kinds of things like mice and spiders and snakes and things like that. Heights would be probably the second most common. So that's very common as well. But we also see lots of people with a fear of flying, a fear of enclosed places, blood, needles, a fear of being in water, fear of storms.
因为节目里可能会出现蛇，或者你因为做关于蛇的噩梦而失眠，那么这种程度可能就被称为恐惧症了。蛇是常见的恐惧症之一，那么还有哪些常见的恐惧症？它们的诱因是什么？它们之间有什么特别的联系吗？是的，最常见的恐惧症很可能是动物恐惧症。比如蜘蛛、蛇、狗、猫、鸟、老鼠。在动物恐惧症中，最常见的是对那些令人毛骨悚然的小动物的恐惧，比如老鼠、蜘蛛和蛇等。恐高症可能是第二常见的，也非常普遍。此外，我们还看到很多人患有飞行恐惧症、幽闭恐惧症、血液恐惧症、针头恐惧症、恐水症和风暴恐惧症。

7
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:51.000
So lots of different things, but those are some of the more common ones. And all of these, if you're phobic, it impairs your life, right? I mean, it's not just that you're worried that a hurricane is coming because it really is coming, but that you spend a lot of time dwelling on this, ruminating over, there's going to be a storm. Oh, my God, I can't go anywhere. Yeah, so some of the people I've seen who come for treatment of storm phobias, it's affecting their life in a few ways. So, first of all, they're constantly monitoring the weather channel, for example, and on days that there are possible storms or even just maybe rain forecast. They may not go to work on those days.
所以种类繁多，但这些是比较常见的。如果患有恐惧症，它会损害你的生活，对吧？我的意思是，这不仅仅是因为飓风真的要来了而担心，而是你花了很多时间纠结于此，反复思虑“会有风暴，天哪，我哪儿也去不了”。是的，我见过一些来治疗风暴恐惧症的患者，这在几个方面影响了他们的生活。首先，他们会不断监测天气预报，在可能出现风暴甚至只是预报有雨的日子里，他们可能就不会去上班了。

8
00:04:51.000 --> 00:05:20.000
And then when there is a storm, they may have to hide in their basement so they don't see the, lightning through the window and they have the music blaring so they don't hear the thunder. So it affects their ability to make plans and do the things that they want to do. And yeah, so it's affecting their life. And that's what brings people in for help. Where do phobias come from? Do people just have a bad experience at some point early in life, like say a turbulent flight that then leads to fear of flying?
当风暴真的来临时，他们可能不得不躲在地下室，这样就看不到窗外的闪电，还要把音乐开得很大声，这样就听不到雷声。这影响了他们制定计划和做自己想做事情的能力。是的，这确实影响了他们的生活，这也是人们寻求帮助的原因。恐惧症从何而来？人们是否只是在生命早期的某个时刻有过糟糕的经历，比如一次颠簸的飞行导致了飞行恐惧症？

9
00:05:20.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Or are the origins of phobia less clear cut than that? Yeah, so I think it really depends on the person. So different people have different pathways to developing fear. So three pathways that have been identified. One is a negative experience in the situation. So people have a direct learning experience. So maybe they've been bitten by a dog or been in a car accident that leads to a fear of dogs or fear of driving. But we can also develop phobias through modeling. And modeling basically just involves watching other people. So learning through observations. So if you You grow up with parents who are terrified of driving and you see their fear year after year, you know, by the time you get your license, you know, you might be at a higher likelihood of having a fear of driving just from watching them be afraid for so long. So we know that children often learn to be afraid by watching other people's responses and situations. A third pathway is through information or instruction. And this is learning through language, things that people tell. us. So if our parents are always warning us to be careful when we're driving because, you know,
还是说恐惧症的起源并不那么明确？是的，我认为这确实因人而异。不同的人有不同的恐惧形成路径。目前已确定了三种路径。第一种是情境中的负面经历，即人们有直接的学习体验。比如，可能被狗咬过或经历过车祸，从而导致对狗或驾驶的恐惧。但我们也可以通过“模仿”产生恐惧症。模仿基本上就是观察他人，即通过观察来学习。如果你在父母对驾驶极度恐惧的环境中长大，年复一年地看到他们的恐惧，那么当你拿到驾照时，你因为长期观察他们的恐惧行为，患上驾驶恐惧症的可能性就会更高。我们知道，孩子们往往通过观察他人在特定情境下的反应来学会恐惧。第三种路径是通过信息或指导，这是通过语言学习，即别人告诉我们的事情。比如，如果我们的父母总是警告我们要小心驾驶，因为“你永远不知道会发生什么”。或者媒体当然也会导致人们产生各种恐惧。这可能是形成恐惧的第三种路径。但我还要补充一点，这些路径对每个人的影响并不相同。两个人可能都经历过车祸，一个人产生了驾驶恐惧症，而另一些人则没有。所以我们知道，还有其他因素，比如遗传，会增加我们在经历这些事件后产生恐惧的脆弱性。

10
00:06:31.000 --> 00:07:07.000
you never know what's going to happen. Or the media certainly can contribute to different fears that people have. That could be a third pathway to developing fear. So but I'll just add, those pathways don't affect everybody equally. So two people can have a car accident. One person develops to fear of driving and one doesn't. So we know that there are also other factors, things with genetics, for example, that increase our vulnerability to developing fear following one of these events.
（注：此段落原文在第8段末尾与第9段开头有重叠，已按逻辑顺延）

11
00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:44.000
And the last thing I want to mention is the role of what we call preparedness. And all that means is that through evolution, presumably we are prepared or predisposed to learn to fear some things more, easily than others. So just as an example, almost nobody has a fear of flowers. But it's very common to have fears of spiders and snakes. And the thinking is that spiders and snakes prehistoricly would have been more dangerous to our ancestors than flowers would be. Even modern threats,
我想提到的最后一点是所谓的“准备性”（preparedness）的作用。这意味着通过进化，我们可能已经准备好或预先倾向于更容易学会对某些事物产生恐惧。举个例子，几乎没有人会害怕花朵，但害怕蜘蛛和蛇却非常普遍。人们认为，在史前时代，蜘蛛和蛇对我们的祖先来说比花朵更危险。即使是现代威胁，

12
00:07:44.000 --> 00:08:28.000
like an electrical socket, theoretically, electrical sockets can be dangerous. But we don't see a lot of people with phobias of electrical sockets. And again, the thinking is that we haven't developed that, kind of put that propensity to develop that fear through evolution because they just haven't been around for that long. So there's our learning plays a role, but so does our genetics and so does just how we're built in terms of how we've evolved to be more careful around things. that are potentially threatening and things that have been threatening for thousands and tens of thousands and hundreds of thousands of years.
比如电源插座，理论上电源插座也可能很危险，但我们并没有看到很多人患有电源插座恐惧症。同样，人们认为这是因为我们还没有通过进化形成那种产生恐惧的倾向，因为它们出现的时间还不够长。所以，学习起着一定作用，但遗传以及我们如何进化以对潜在威胁事物保持警惕，同样起着作用。这些威胁在成千上万年甚至几十万年的时间里一直存在。

13
00:08:28.000 --> 00:09:10.000
So let's talk about treatment for a minute. What are some of the ways that you can help people overcome phobias? What is based on scientific evidence that has been shown to work? So the most effective treatment is exposure therapy. So we have all benefited from exposure to overcome a fear of one thing or another. One thing I would ask the audience to do is to reflect on is there anything in their life that they used to be afraid of that they're not afraid of anymore. And it might be maybe when there were kids, they were afraid of the dark and now they're not. Or maybe the first day at work at their job, they were very nervous meeting new people.
让我们谈谈治疗。有哪些方法可以帮助人们克服恐惧症？哪些是基于科学证据并被证明有效的？最有效的治疗方法是暴露疗法。我们都曾受益于暴露疗法来克服某种恐惧。我想请听众反思一下，生活中是否有曾经让你害怕但现在不再害怕的事情。也许小时候你怕黑，但现在不怕了；或者第一天上班时，你对结识新同事感到非常紧张。

14
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:28.000
Or the first time they went on a date with their partner, they were very nervous. And it just got easier over time. And I might ask them, why did it get easier? And usually the answer is, well, I just had to do it. And it got easier. I got used to it. I learned that the situation wasn't dangerous. And that's what exposure therapy is all about.
或者第一次和伴侣约会时，你非常紧张。但随着时间的推移，一切都变得容易了。我可能会问他们，为什么变得容易了？通常答案是：“我不得不去做，然后就变得容易了，我习惯了，我意识到这种情况并不危险。”这就是暴露疗法的核心。

15
00:09:28.000 --> 00:10:04.000
We come up with a plan where people gradually expose themselves to the situation or the object that they're afraid of at their own pace. We don't force people to do anything. And again, it's done gradually. People start at a place what they're willing to. and as they become more comfortable, they try things that are more challenging. So for phobias, that tends to be the main component of treatment. There are other strategies that may be useful as well, but that would be the most important one for specific phobias in particular.
我们制定一个计划，让人们按照自己的节奏逐渐暴露在他们害怕的情境或物体中。我们不会强迫任何人做任何事。同样，这是循序渐进的。人们从他们愿意尝试的地方开始，随着他们变得更加舒适，他们会尝试更具挑战性的事情。对于恐惧症来说，这往往是治疗的主要组成部分。虽然还有其他策略可能有用，但对于特定恐惧症而言，这是最重要的。

16
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:33.000
Now, with exposure therapy, is that something you can do on your own, or do you need a professional to help you? That is a great question. So I get asked all the time, if I read your... your self-help book on how to overcome a phobia, will I overcome it? And my answer is, it's always the same. I say, yes, in the same way that if you read a book on physical fitness, you'll get physically fit. So in other words, reading the book isn't going to get you over your phobia.
那么，暴露疗法是你可以自己做的，还是需要专业人士的帮助？这是一个很好的问题。人们经常问我：“如果我读了你写的关于如何克服恐惧症的自助书，我能克服它吗？”我的回答总是相同的。我说：“可以，就像如果你读了一本关于健身的书，你就能变得健康一样。”换句话说，光读这本书并不能让你克服恐惧症。

17
00:10:33.000 --> 00:11:09.000
You have to do all the things that the book describes. And some people are able to do that. I get emails from people all over the world who said that the strategies in the book change their lives. but we also know from research that many people cannot do it on their own or completely on their own. They need the support of a therapist who can help to motivate them, help to solve any problems that come up. In the case of animal phobia, you often need someone else to be controlling the animal and holding the animals because the client who's afraid of dogs can't go out and find dogs themselves
你必须去做书中描述的所有事情。有些人能够做到。我收到过来自世界各地人们的电子邮件，说书中的策略改变了他们的生活。但我们也从研究中得知，许多人无法完全靠自己做到。他们需要治疗师的支持，治疗师可以帮助激励他们，帮助解决出现的任何问题。在动物恐惧症的情况下，你通常需要另一个人来控制动物，因为害怕狗的客户自己无法去寻找狗，

18
00:11:09.000 --> 00:12:05.000
or spiders or whatever. So having somebody there, whether it's a family member or therapist, to help control the environment and offer support, can be useful. So I guess the quick answer is some people are able to do on their own. Other people need the support of a therapist or family member, somebody to work with them in the situation. Are there some phobias that are harder to get over than others, or does it really just depend on the individual who has the phobia? Yeah. I think it depends a lot on the individual. So I've seen lots of people with the same fear who respond differently to treatment. It does, one thing I would say is that the type of fear does impact on how long it takes to get over it. So there are studies with animal phobias showing that in about two to three hours of exposure, even in a single session, the majority of people can overcome the fear.
或者蜘蛛之类的。所以，有个人在场，无论是家人还是治疗师，来帮助控制环境并提供支持，都是有用的。所以我想简短的回答是：有些人可以自己做到，而另一些人则需要治疗师或家人的支持，需要有人在情境中与他们一起工作。有些恐惧症是否比其他恐惧症更难克服，还是说这完全取决于患者个人？是的，我认为这很大程度上取决于个人。我见过许多患有同样恐惧症的人对治疗的反应不同。有一点我想说，恐惧的类型确实会影响克服它所需的时间。有研究表明，对于动物恐惧症，在两到三个小时的暴露治疗中，即使是在单次疗程中，大多数人也能克服恐惧。

19
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:48.000
In my experience with driving fears, it takes longer. So it might take me about 10 weeks with somebody where they're practicing a lot in between sessions as well to get to that same point. The other thing I would say is that although most people with a specific phobia can overcome the fear through treatment, more complex kinds of problems like social anxiety where people are nervous in a wide range of social situations. that can take longer to overcome. And it's not unusual for people to be doing much better at the end of treatment, but still struggling with social anxiety from time to time. So it's often a less complete recovery than it would be with something like an animal phobia or a height phobia.
根据我的经验，驾驶恐惧症需要更长的时间。我可能需要花大约 10 周的时间，让患者在疗程之间进行大量练习，才能达到同样的程度。另外我想说的是，虽然大多数患有特定恐惧症的人可以通过治疗克服恐惧，但像社交焦虑这样更复杂的问题，即人们在广泛的社交场合感到紧张，可能需要更长的时间才能克服。治疗结束时，人们的情况好转很多，但偶尔仍会受到社交焦虑的困扰，这种情况并不罕见。因此，与动物恐惧症或恐高症相比，这种恢复往往不那么彻底。

20
00:12:48.000 --> 00:13:23.000
I'm curious about fear of flying as one example, because, I mean, you could go through a lot of the steps that you outline in your books and your manuals. And, I mean, it just seems really tough if you get to the point where you're on an airplane and you have a panic attack, essentially, you can't get off the plane. I mean, how do you work up to getting over fear of flying, which can be very intense for people? Yeah, so people are afraid of flying for different reasons. So for some people, it's a fear of the plane crashing. They're not afraid of having a panic attack. They're afraid of the plane itself.
我对飞行恐惧症很好奇，因为你可以按照书中的步骤进行，但如果你到了飞机上并产生恐慌发作，基本上你就无法离开飞机了。对于那些对飞行有强烈恐惧的人，你是如何帮助他们克服的？是的，人们害怕飞行有不同的原因。对一些人来说，这是对飞机坠毁的恐惧，他们并不害怕恐慌发作，他们害怕的是飞机本身。

21
00:13:23.000 --> 00:14:05.000
For other people, they are afraid of having a panic attack. So I've had, you know, one person said when I'm, when I'm on a plane and I'm feeling frightened, I wish the plane would crash. You know, for that person, it's not a fear of the plane. It's a fear of how they feel on the plane. So again, having a panic attack and not being able to escape. And if that's the case, we can often treat people on the ground. Because people who are afraid of panicking are also afraid of other situations often where they might panic and not be able to get out, like an enclosed place, for example. and they're afraid of the panic symptoms themselves. So we can encourage people to experience those symptoms, hyperventilate, you know, breathing really quickly, for example,
